1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,764
Can you show me environment variables?

2
00:00:08,300 --> 00:00:14,775
The same image must run in development, staging and production with different settings:

3
00:00:14,775 --> 00:00:17,678
ports, URLs, feature flags, log levels.

4
00:00:17,678 --> 00:00:23,632
The standard way to hand those settings to a container is environment variables,

5
00:00:23,632 --> 00:00:26,758
set with -e when the container is created.

6
00:00:26,758 --> 00:00:33,085
The image can define defaults (ENV in the Dockerfile, lesson 030); -e overrides them.

7
00:00:34,833 --> 00:00:38,793
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

8
00:00:39,333 --> 00:00:47,471
Every container already has some variables, from Docker (HOSTNAME) and from the image (PATH, NODE_VERSION):

9
00:00:47,471 --> 00:00:51,958
Run the API with no configuration, then with configuration:

10
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:57,029
Run the API with no configuration, then with configuration:

11
00:00:57,029 --> 00:01:00,329
Same code, same image, different behaviour.

12
00:01:00,329 --> 00:01:04,934
-e NAME without a value copies the variable from your shell:

13
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:08,877
Same code, same image, different behaviour.

14
00:01:08,877 --> 00:01:13,590
-e NAME without a value copies the variable from your shell:

15
00:01:15,333 --> 00:01:17,007
Let me try something.

16
00:01:17,007 --> 00:01:21,153
Start a database without its required configuration:

17
00:01:21,700 --> 00:01:25,604
The container exited with status 1 a moment after starting.

18
00:01:25,604 --> 00:01:30,169
A container that exits immediately has written the reason to its log:

19
00:01:31,933 --> 00:01:38,733
Recreate the container with the variable (a container's environment cannot be changed after it was created):

20
00:01:40,467 --> 00:01:41,180
Got it.

21
00:01:41,180 --> 00:01:43,931
What do I need to remember?

22
00:01:44,467 --> 00:01:49,270
Configuration goes into the container as environment variables:

23
00:01:49,270 --> 00:01:52,396
-e NAME=value, or -e NAME from the shell.

24
00:01:52,933 --> 00:01:59,218
-e overrides ENV defaults of the image; a container's environment is fixed when it is created.

25
00:01:59,767 --> 00:02:04,351
A container that exits immediately often lacks a required variable:

26
00:02:04,351 --> 00:02:06,266
docker logs tells you which.

27
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,496
I keep hearing about environment files.

28
00:02:11,496 --> 00:02:13,199
What is it, really?

29
00:02:13,733 --> 00:02:20,401
Ten -e flags make an unreadable command and leak values into your shell history.

30
00:02:20,401 --> 00:02:22,568
An env file collects them:

31
00:02:22,568 --> 00:02:28,402
one NAME=value per line, comments with #, passed with --env-file FILE.

32
00:02:28,402 --> 00:02:36,153
One file per environment (dev.env, staging.env) keeps configuration versioned and reviewable.

33
00:02:37,900 --> 00:02:41,859
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

34
00:02:42,400 --> 00:02:43,889
Pass the whole file:

35
00:02:43,889 --> 00:02:48,729
The comment line was ignored; every other line became a variable:

36
00:02:49,267 --> 00:02:53,559
The comment line was ignored; every other line became a variable:

37
00:02:53,559 --> 00:02:57,191
-e on the command line overrides a value from the file:

38
00:02:57,733 --> 00:03:01,343
-e on the command line overrides a value from the file:

39
00:03:03,100 --> 00:03:04,701
Let me try something.

40
00:03:04,701 --> 00:03:10,264
Someone "cleans up" the file in the style of a shell script, with quotes:

41
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:13,646
The greeting now contains the quotes:

42
00:03:13,646 --> 00:03:16,569
"\"Welcome to the cafe\"" in the JSON.

43
00:03:16,569 --> 00:03:20,799
Look at the value exactly as the container received it:

44
00:03:22,533 --> 00:03:27,818
Write values without quotes; spaces inside a value need no quoting:

45
00:03:29,567 --> 00:03:30,280
Got it.

46
00:03:30,280 --> 00:03:33,031
What do I need to remember?

47
00:03:33,567 --> 00:03:39,566
--env-file FILE passes many variables at once; # lines are comments.

48
00:03:40,100 --> 00:03:41,793
Values are literal:

49
00:03:41,793 --> 00:03:47,049
no quotes, no $ expansion; quotes become part of the value.

50
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:52,279
Later files override earlier ones; -e overrides any file.

51
00:03:54,033 --> 00:03:57,428
How does configuration vs secrets work?

52
00:03:57,967 --> 00:04:06,010
Configuration (a log level, a host name, a feature flag) may be seen by anyone who operates the application.

53
00:04:06,010 --> 00:04:12,415
A secret (a password, an API key, a private key) must be seen only by the application.

54
00:04:12,415 --> 00:04:17,330
Environment variables are fine for configuration but leak secrets:

55
00:04:17,330 --> 00:04:23,586
they appear in docker inspect, in the environment of every process in the container,

56
00:04:23,586 --> 00:04:27,086
and in crash reports that dump the environment.

57
00:04:28,833 --> 00:04:32,793
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

58
00:04:33,333 --> 00:04:37,838
First the common way, a password in an environment variable:

59
00:04:38,400 --> 00:04:40,054
Now the secret-file way:

60
00:04:40,054 --> 00:04:45,569
the file is mounted read-only, and the image is told where to read the password:

61
00:04:46,133 --> 00:04:50,183
The password from the file works for a login over the network:

62
00:04:51,933 --> 00:04:53,393
Let me try something.

63
00:04:53,393 --> 00:04:58,330
A teammate is asked for the container configuration to debug a problem,

64
00:04:58,330 --> 00:05:01,598
and pastes docker inspect output into a ticket:

65
00:05:02,133 --> 00:05:06,352
An environment variable is part of the container's configuration,

66
00:05:06,352 --> 00:05:09,403
so everything that reads configuration sees it:

67
00:05:09,403 --> 00:05:16,023
docker inspect, anyone in the docker group, monitoring agents, and every process inside the container:

68
00:05:17,767 --> 00:05:21,081
Recreate the leaky container with the secret file,

69
00:05:21,081 --> 00:05:25,191
then rotate the leaked password (it must be considered known):

70
00:05:26,933 --> 00:05:27,647
Got it.

71
00:05:27,647 --> 00:05:30,398
What do I need to remember?

72
00:05:30,933 --> 00:05:34,851
Configuration may be visible; secrets must not be.

73
00:05:34,851 --> 00:05:38,298
Treat every environment variable as visible.

74
00:05:38,833 --> 00:05:44,363
-e secrets leak through docker inspect, process environments and crash dumps.

75
00:05:44,900 --> 00:05:54,294
Pass secrets as read-only files (/run/secrets/NAME) and pass only their path as configuration (*_FILE).
